    en: {
      title: '66 million litres: what the State spent on bottled water',
      dek: 'During the 2023 drought the State secured the purchase of over 66 million litres of water for US$25 million. The opposition and the union said it bought late and dear.',
      contexto: 'On 19 June 2023, with tap water salinised across the metropolitan area, President Luis Lacalle Pou declared a water emergency. According to the Presidency\'s statement, the government announced the removal of taxes on bottled water by decree and bill, the delivery of two free litres of water a day to some 21,000 Mides beneficiaries, guaranteed supply to hospitals, care homes, CAIF centres and INAU facilities, and the start of urgent works, among them a reservoir on the San José river. According to El Observador, the VAT (22%) and Imesi (10.5%) exemption was set in late June 2023, and the water-purchase coverage for vulnerable populations ran until 11 September 2023.',
      hallazgo: 'El Observador reported on 23 August 2023 that the government secured the purchase of more than 66 million litres of bottled water for vulnerable populations, an investment of US$25 million. Earlier, in July, la diaria had gathered criticism from the opposition and OSE\'s union: the utility\'s Frente Amplio director, Edgardo Ortuño, held that "these emergency works became more expensive because they were raised late" and that in that time "we could have processed the purchases at lower cost", citing as an example a pipe purchase of some 20 million. The argument was that had the purchases been made when the agricultural emergency was declared in October 2022, or when restrictions began in February 2023, they would have cost considerably less. The same article recorded that OSE would face the following years with its budget in the red and that the FFOSE union reported staffing below the necessary minimum.',
      statusNote: 'According to El Observador, with the end of the water emergency the president signed the decree to lift it and bottled water again paid 22% VAT and 10.5% Imesi per litre. Edgardo Ortuño, then the opposition director at OSE and a critic of the late purchasing, is today Environment Minister in Yamandú Orsi\'s government. As of this file\'s closing (August 2026) no audit rulings after August 2023 on these specific purchases are on record for this file.',
      porQueImporta: 'This was US$25 million spent buying the water the public system could not deliver, on top of the tax revenue forgone through the exemption. The cost of having no reserve is not paid only in works: it is also paid in bottles, and every taxpayer pays it.',
      caveat: 'The purchases were made under an emergency regime and by different bodies (Mides, OSE, the Presidency), so in the feed they may appear scattered, under a generic description, or through exceptions to open tendering. The US$25 million total is the figure reported by the press. The criticism of "late and expensive" purchasing comes from the opposition and the union, not from an audit finding.',
    },
